eresting is  apparently common in jail another  example is the deck of pain here you go  through a pack of cards one at the time  performing a different movement for each  suit and reps depending on the number of  reps so it's a kind of chaos trading  where you never know exactly what you're  gonna get dealt next this is an  interesting aspect of training and  isolation finding ways to keep the  training fresh and motivating without a  coach shouting in your ear or tunes  coming through your headphones it's a  form of gamification which is a great  strategy for anyone who struggles with  motivation when training body weight is  one obvious option when it comes to  training without gym access but there  are other options too for example  overcoming isometrics  I've discussed this topic before but  essentially the aim is to push or pull  against an immovable force by pushing  against a wall or pulling against the  tree which  by the way was another method used by  gamma it's possible to engage a hundred  percent of your strength as far as the  body is concerned it's the same as  trying to lift or pull more than 100  percent of your one rep maximum and once  again the body will adapt in the same  way increasing your mind muscle  connection and neural drive to help you  engage more muscle fiber and more  strength you may not see as much  hypertrophy due to the lower muscle  damage there's not really any eccentric  portion going on and the lack of full  range of motion might also mean the  strength gains aren't as great however  it also has unique advantages as there  is no strength curve to contend with  for example meaning that a hundred  percent effort is sustained for much  longer than in one rep max it's a great  supplement to strength training and if  you're in jail well then it might be all  you've got you need to do this at at  least three different joint angles  seeing as the area of effect is only  around 30 degrees holding threat for  about six seconds you can